{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Moses went up.</b> The sage Rabbi Abraham, of blessed memory, wrote: in my opinion, from this verse onward Joshua wrote, for after Moses went up he did not come down, and he wrote it by way of prophecy; and the proof is ״ויראהו ה׳״, also ״ויאמר ה׳ אליו״, also ״ויקבר אותו״ — until here. He needed to explain it thus because it seemed difficult to him that Moses should write of his own death and burial. <b>But this is not so</b>, and its explanation is not correct. Rather, the correct belief and the true tradition that we hold is that Moses wrote the entire Torah, from בראשית until לעיני כל ישראל, all of it from the mouth of the Almighty. And Moses is like one copying an ancient book, from beginning to end, letter by letter. And this is the opinion of Rabbi Meir, who said: is it possible that a Torah scroll is missing even one letter, and Moses says, ״Take this book of the Torah״? Rather, the Holy One, blessed be He, speaks and Moses writes in tears. And it seems to me that there is no reason to wonder if Moses writes ״וימת שם משה עבד ה׳״ and ״ויקבר אותו בגי״ while he is yet alive, for he was writing what was destined to be. And so above, in the words of the Song (Deuteronomy 32): ״וירא ה׳ וינאץ מכעס בניו ובנותיו״ — by this He made known what was destined to be in the First Temple; and so too in other matters, in the verses of the future, where it speaks in the past tense — for all the prophets follow this practice in their words, to speak in the past tense in place of the future."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 34, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:54:32.583799019Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}